@startuml D3_Flow3_Commands_Skills_Subagents
' ============================================================
' DOMAIN 3 — FLOW 3: Slash Commands + Skills + Subagents
' (Topics 12–17) organised as the 3-LAYER INSTRUCTION STACK.
' CORE IDEA: CLAUDE.md (always on) < SKILLS (description-gated)
'   < SUBAGENTS (autonomous workers). Commands are a SUBSET
'   of skills (manual-only loading).
' MATCHING SIGNAL = the DESCRIPTION (same routing idea as D2).
' ============================================================
title Domain 3 · Flow 3 — Commands, Skills & Subagents (T12–17)\n(The instruction stack: always-on -> description-gated -> autonomous)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF6CC

start
:A request arrives in a Claude Code session;

' ---------- THE INSTRUCTION STACK (framing) ----------
partition "The 3-Layer Instruction Stack" {
  #LightCyan:Place each mechanism by HOW it loads;
  note right
    BOTTOM  CLAUDE.md  — ALWAYS in context, applies everywhere,
                         no matching, no invocation.
    MIDDLE  SKILLS     — DESCRIPTION-GATED. Out of context until
                         the description matches, THEN body loads.
    TOP     SUBAGENTS  — autonomous WORKERS you spin up; active
                         processes with their OWN context + tools.
    ----
    Skill = the recipe; Claude Code = the cook.
    Subagent = a separate worker who cooks independently.
    Choosing the right layer prevents over-engineering.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 12: SLASH COMMANDS ----------
partition "T12 · Custom Slash Commands (saved shortcut prompts)" {
  :Create a markdown file under .claude/commands/;
  #LightGreen:FILE NAME = the keyword\n(deploy-review.md -> /deploy-review);
  note right
    A slash command = a keyword mapped to a markdown file
    Claude reads as a PROMPT. No registration/config step.
    Body = role + task + output format + constraints.
    ARGUMENTS placeholder makes it dynamic:
    "review PR $ARGUMENTS" + `/review-pr 4521` -> 4521 injected.
    ----
    SCOPE (by file location, like CLAUDE.md):
    * project .claude/commands/ = committed -> whole team
    * user  ~/.claude/commands/ = personal, all projects
    Built-ins (/init, /help, /code-review) ship with the tool —
    reusing a built-in NAME shadows it. Type "/" to see the list.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 13: COMMAND DESIGN + DISTRIBUTION ----------
partition "T13 · Command Design & Team Distribution" {
  :Design: NARROW SCOPE + VERB-NOUN names;
  note right
    #1 rule: one command does ONE thing.
    Anti-pattern: /do-everything (review+refactor+test+deploy)
    -> works occasionally, fails confusingly.
    Verb-noun (/review-pr, /run-tests, /explain-file) is
    self-documenting: guess the keyword before reading docs.
    ----
    DISTRIBUTION: project = commit the folder (channel already
    exists, travels with code) · user = personal ·
    MANAGED SETTINGS = admins push to every user org-wide;
    users can't delete/override.
    2nd anti-pattern: DON'T duplicate CLAUDE.md rules inside a
    command -> they drift out of sync. Extend, don't repeat.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 14: WHAT SKILLS ARE ----------
partition "T14 · What Skills Are & When to Build Them" {
  if (Should this load AUTOMATICALLY when relevant?) then (YES)
    #LightGreen:SKILL — description-matched, loads itself;
    note right
      Skill = reusable instruction package (skill.md + optional
      files). Claude reads the DESCRIPTION and pulls it in when
      it matches — no need to remember to invoke.
      e.g. a release procedure: bump version, update changelog,
      tag commit, draft notes -> "cut a release" auto-loads it.
      Custom commands are now a SUBSET of skills:
      one mechanism, two loading patterns —
      manual-only (you type it) vs auto (description matches).
    end note
  else (NO — type it by hand)
    #LightYellow:Manual-only command (a skill you invoke explicitly);
  endif
  :Decide with FREQUENCY · COMPLEXITY · REUSABILITY;
  note right
    Build a skill when 2+ hold: recurring multi-step workflow,
    team-wide convention, should auto-load, reusable package.
    DON'T build for: one-time tasks, simple lookups, or rules
    that should ALWAYS apply (-> those belong in CLAUDE.md).
    High on all 3 factors -> strong skill candidate.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 15: AUTHORING SKILLS ----------
partition "T15 · Authoring Agent Skills (directory + front matter)" {
  :Structure: .claude/skills/<name>/skill.md;
  note right
    A skill is a DIRECTORY, not a loose file.
    Folder name = the skill's name & how you invoke it directly.
    Discovery is AUTOMATIC — no registry, no manifest.
    ----
    EXAM TRAP: front matter (the top YAML block) is ENTIRELY
    OPTIONAL. Every field optional. A skill with NO front matter
    still works — the BODY always loads. No silent failure.
    Body = what to do; front matter = when to fire + conditions.
  end note
  #LightCyan:Write the DESCRIPTION carefully — it drives matching;
  note right
    DESCRIPTION = the whole auto-invocation mechanism
    (same routing signal as a TOOL description in D2).
    Vague -> skill passed over; sharp -> fires reliably.
    Other real fields (all optional): allowed-tools (PRE-APPROVES,
    others still available) vs disallowed-tools (actually REMOVES
    tools) — ASYMMETRIC; model/effort; paths; context: fork (+agent)
    to run in an isolated sub-agent; argument-hint.
    ----
    INVENTED fields that do NOTHING: trigger, version, author/tags.
    Two invocation paths: AUTO (description match) · DIRECT (folder name).
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 16–17: SUBAGENTS ----------
partition "T16–17 · Subagents (delegated autonomous workers)" {
  if (Need a WORKER to run something independently / in parallel?) then (YES)
    #LightGreen:Spin up a SUBAGENT (own context, tools, scope);
    note right
      Subagent = active process, NOT a passive template.
      It runs independently and REPORTS BACK when done.
      Turns Claude Code into an ORCHESTRATOR.
      Use for: tasks too big for the current window, or
      isolated enough to run in parallel.
      Packages vs Workers = Skills vs Subagents.
    end note
    :Design for delegation:\nclear scope · bounded authority · isolation · structured return;
    note right
      Delegation design: give a narrow, well-defined task and
      the minimum tools it needs. Return findings as a STRUCTURED
      payload; NEVER swallow errors — propagate them up so the
      orchestrator can react. (Pairs with plan mode: subagent
      investigates -> main agent turns findings into a plan.)
    end note
  else (NO — just need instructions to follow)
    #LightYellow:A skill or CLAUDE.md rule is enough;
  endif
}

stop

floating note left
  == FLOW 3 SPINE (the stack) ==
  CLAUDE.md (always on)  <  SKILLS (description-gated)  <  SUBAGENTS (workers)
  Commands = manual-only SKILLS (filename/foldername = keyword).
  ----
  == HIGH-YIELD FACTS ==
  * Command/skill/subagent = load AUTOMATICALLY? no->cmd, yes->skill,
    or need a worker? -> subagent
  * Skill = a DIRECTORY; front matter OPTIONAL; body always loads
  * DESCRIPTION drives matching (same as D2 tool routing)
  * allowed-tools pre-approves; disallowed-tools removes (asymmetric)
  * No trigger/version/author fields
  * Narrow scope + verb-noun names; don't duplicate CLAUDE.md
  * Managed settings = org-wide command push
  * Subagents: own context, structured returns, never swallow errors
end note

@enduml
